# Passing Variables from PHP to HTML: The URL Query Parameter Pattern

The core issue in your current implementation stems from a fundamental misunderstanding of how server-side languages communicate with client-side documents. You cannot simply inject a PHP variable directly into an HTML file using syntax like `src="$mymp3"`. HTML files are static text; they do not execute PHP code unless explicitly processed by the web server before being sent to the browser.

When your `play.php` script executes, it sets a header and calls `exit`. This tells the browser to stop loading the current document (`play.html`) and immediately load the URL specified in the location header. The browser then requests `player.html?mp3name=/MP3/1.mp3`. At this specific moment, `player.html` is requested as a static resource. If the file contains `$mymp3`, the browser receives the literal characters `$mymp3`, not the value `/MP3/1.mp3`.

To pass data from PHP to HTML, you must rely on the URL itself. The standard mechanism for this is the query string. Your `play.php` script already does exactly this by redirecting to `$playerurl?mp3name=$mymp3`. This embeds the variable into the request URI. Consequently, the receiving page (`player.html`) must read this data from the `$_GET` superglobal array, not try to define it as a local PHP variable inside the HTML tag.

## Reading Query Parameters in Static HTML

The primary challenge here is that you are trying to make a static HTML file behave dynamically based on URL parameters. Since `player.html` is served as a static file (likely via `.htaccess` or your web server configuration), it cannot contain PHP tags unless that specific file is routed through the PHP interpreter.

If `player.html` remains a static `.html` file, you have two distinct architectural paths to solve this: using JavaScript to resolve the parameter at runtime, or converting the file to a PHP script.

### Approach A: Using JavaScript in Static HTML

If you must keep `player.html` as a pure HTML file, you cannot use PHP tags to read the variable. Instead, you must extract the query parameter on the client side using JavaScript. The `URLSearchParams` API is the modern, robust way to handle this.

Here is how you modify the `<audio>` tag in `player.html`:

```html
<script>
    // Parse the URL to get the 'mp3name' query parameter
    const urlParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
    const mp3Name = urlParams.get('mp3name');

    // Only proceed if a valid file path was passed
    if (mp3Name) {
        document.getElementById('player').src = mp3Name;

        // Optional: Trigger autoplay only if the user interaction policy allows it immediately,
        // otherwise rely on the Play button as you have implemented.
        // document.getElementById('player').play(); 
    } else {
        console.error('No MP3 file specified in URL');
    }
</script>

<audio id="player" src="" autoplay preload="auto">
    Your browser does not support the audio element.
</audio>

<div>
    <button onclick="document.getElementById('player').play()">Play</button>
    <button onclick="document.getElementById('player').pause()">Pause</button>
    <button onclick="document.getElementById('player').volume = Math.min(1, document.getElementById('player').volume + 0.1)">Volume Up</button>
    <button onclick="document.getElementById('player').volume = Math.max(0, document.getElementById('player').volume - 0.1)">Volume Down</button>
</div>
```

This approach decouples the server logic from the client rendering. The `play.php` script handles the heavy lifting of determining which file to play and passing that intent via the URL. The static HTML simply interprets that intent once it loads.

### Approach B: Converting to a PHP Script

If you require more complex logic within `player.html`âsuch as sanitizing the input or handling different error states using server-side directivesâyou should rename the file to `player.php`. When the web server routes requests ending in `.php`, they are passed to the interpreter.

In this scenario, your `player.php` file would look like this:

```php
<?php
// Define the base URL for the player interface
$playerurl = "player.php"; 

// Retrieve the variable passed from play.php
$mymp3 = $_GET['mp3name'] ?? null;

// Validate that the file path exists and is safe before using it
if ($mymp3 && !empty($mymp3)) {
    ?>
    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <html lang="en">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <title>Audio Player</title>
    </head>
    <body>
        <audio id="player" src="<?php echo htmlspecialchars($mymp3); ?>" autoplay preload="auto">
            Browser does not support audio.
        </audio>

        <div>
            <button onclick="document.getElementById('player').play()">Play</button>
            <button onclick="document.getElementById('player').pause()">Pause</button>
            <button onclick="document.getElementById('player').volume = Math.min(1, document.getElementById('player').volume + 0.1)">Volume Up</button>
            <button onclick="document.getElementById('player').volume = Math.max(0, document.getElementById('player').volume - 0.1)">Volume Down</button>
        </div>
    </body>
    </html>
    <?php
} else {
    die("Error: No valid MP3 file name provided.");
}
?>
```

This method offers better control over output encoding. Notice the use of `htmlspecialchars()`. Since `$mymp3` comes from user input (via the form), passing it directly into HTML attributes poses a Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) risk. Using this function ensures that special characters like `<`, `>`, or `&` are escaped, preventing potential injection attacks if a filename contains malicious script tags.

## Handling Autoplay Restrictions

There is a significant trade-off to consider regarding the `autoplay` attribute in your original code. Modern browsers enforce strict policies against autoplaying audio without user interaction. If the page loads via a direct link or a redirect that doesn't count as an immediate user gesture, the browser will likely silence the audio and throw a console error.

In the static HTML approach using JavaScript, you might find that `autoplay` fails silently. The recommended pattern is to set `preload="auto"` (which your code already does) but remove the `autoplay` attribute entirely. Instead, rely on the "Play" button to trigger playback via the JavaScript event listener attached to it. This ensures the browser considers the play action a direct user gesture, bypassing autoplay restrictions while maintaining full functionality for your volume controls and pause/resume logic.

For projects requiring robust routing and cleaner separation of concerns, platforms like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) often demonstrate how to manage these state transitions more elegantly using server-side controllers rather than relying on simple GET parameter parsing in static views.
